1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,472
The Curiosity Club.

2
00:00:02,472 --> 00:00:04,945
Episode 32.

3
00:00:04,945 --> 00:00:07,880
A Desert with No Sandcastle.

4
00:00:07,880 --> 00:00:10,774
Here is something to listen for.

5
00:00:10,774 --> 00:00:14,008
Must every desert be hot and sandy?

6
00:00:14,008 --> 00:00:15,529
Ready?

7
00:00:15,529 --> 00:00:17,635
Let us find out.

8
00:00:17,635 --> 00:00:21,127
The geography club is playing Guess the Place.

9
00:00:21,127 --> 00:00:24,741
Maya reads the first clue: It is a desert.

10
00:00:24,741 --> 00:00:27,921
Leo draws a hot Sun and a camel.

11
00:00:27,921 --> 00:00:31,480
Pip adds a sandcastle with a robot flag.

12
00:00:31,480 --> 00:00:34,307
Second clue, says Maya.

13
00:00:34,307 --> 00:00:37,282
This place can be extremely cold.

14
00:00:37,282 --> 00:00:40,271
Leo puts a scarf on the camel.

15
00:00:40,271 --> 00:00:44,578
Third clue: Much of the land is covered by a thick ice sheet.

16
00:00:44,578 --> 00:00:47,255
Pip looks at his sandcastle.

17
00:00:47,255 --> 00:00:50,353
My building material may be wrong.

18
00:00:50,353 --> 00:00:53,980
The answer is Antarctica, Maya announces.

19
00:00:53,980 --> 00:00:56,331
Leo is surprised.

20
00:00:56,331 --> 00:00:59,687
How can a place with so much ice be a desert?

21
00:00:59,687 --> 00:01:06,399
Ms. Park explains that desert conditions are about very low precipitation, not simply heat or sand.

22
00:01:06,399 --> 00:01:10,081
Much of Antarctica receives little new snowfall.

23
00:01:10,081 --> 00:01:14,198
Ice can accumulate and remain over a very long time.

24
00:01:14,198 --> 00:01:20,339
So the amount already stored and the amount arriving each year are different questions, Maya says.

25
00:01:20,339 --> 00:01:23,940
They imagine a jar holding many old coins.

26
00:01:23,940 --> 00:01:29,266
Even if only a few new coins arrive each year, the jar may still contain a lot.

27
00:01:29,266 --> 00:01:33,573
Pip labels the comparison carefully: Ice is not money.

28
00:01:33,573 --> 00:01:36,929
Please do not pay for lunch with a snowball.

29
00:01:36,929 --> 00:01:39,877
Leo changes his first drawing.

30
00:01:39,877 --> 00:01:43,994
The camel stays on a separate page about a hot desert.

31
00:01:43,994 --> 00:01:47,377
Antarctica gets its own icy landscape.

32
00:01:47,377 --> 00:01:54,836
The club's final clue is for every geography question: Check the definition before trusting the first picture in your head.

33
00:01:54,836 --> 00:01:57,853
Now, a little more to discover.

34
00:01:57,853 --> 00:02:02,173
Deserts are characterized by very low precipitation.

35
00:02:02,173 --> 00:02:06,915
They can be hot or cold, and they do not all consist of sand dunes.

36
00:02:06,915 --> 00:02:10,163
Much of Antarctica is a cold desert.

37
00:02:10,163 --> 00:02:16,358
Its large store of ice does not mean that it receives a large amount of new precipitation each year.

38
00:02:16,358 --> 00:02:19,388
Let us hear the main idea again.

39
00:02:19,388 --> 00:02:25,054
Leo first imagined a hot sandy desert, but Antarctica challenged that picture.

40
00:02:25,054 --> 00:02:31,236
The club used the meaning of desert and distinguished stored ice from newly falling precipitation.

41
00:02:31,236 --> 00:02:34,239
Here are three useful expressions.

42
00:02:34,239 --> 00:02:37,282
You can listen, or say them with me.

43
00:02:37,282 --> 00:02:39,796
It is not always hot.

44
00:02:39,796 --> 00:02:42,309
It is not always hot.

45
00:02:42,309 --> 00:02:44,999
What does the word really mean?

46
00:02:44,999 --> 00:02:47,690
What does the word really mean?

47
00:02:47,690 --> 00:02:50,570
Those are two different questions.

48
00:02:50,570 --> 00:02:53,451
Those are two different questions.

49
00:02:53,451 --> 00:02:56,317
Remember our listening question?

50
00:02:56,317 --> 00:02:59,551
Must every desert be hot and sandy?

51
00:02:59,551 --> 00:03:01,739
Here is the answer.

52
00:03:01,739 --> 00:03:03,328
No.

53
00:03:03,328 --> 00:03:08,763
Very low precipitation is the important feature, and deserts can be cold.

54
00:03:08,763 --> 00:03:12,554
And here is a question just for your imagination.

55
00:03:12,554 --> 00:03:18,505
What word makes you picture one familiar example even though other examples are very different?

56
00:03:18,505 --> 00:03:21,671
There is more than one possible answer.

57
00:03:21,671 --> 00:03:24,823
Keep wondering, and see you next time.
